# **One `refresh`, because the trait it restores is an argument rather than part of its name.**
# Sean, 2026-09-18: *I don't like that I have a separate refresh command for each resource, I feel
# like this should be parameterized somehow.* There were two rules, identical but for which column
# they assigned, and the column was the only thing telling them apart.
#
# **`what` is typed as a relation and not as a family**, which is what lets one rule reach a kind
# and a family alike: `{refresh what:scout trait:moving}` denotes one relation, and
# `{refresh what:unit trait:moving}` denotes every member of that family. **The type cannot narrow
# this to the kinds that carry the trait**, because an input's type is fixed and which kinds carry
# a trait depends on the other argument - so the pairing is refused when fired rather than when
# declared, and `offered` shows only the pairs that would work.
#
# **`{assigns ... input:trait ...}` names an input where it named a column.** The trait's name is
# the column's name, so nothing else has to say which column: `moving` restores `scout.moving`
# and `extractor.moving` does not exist, which is the refusal rather than a lookup that fails
# quietly.

# **`refresh` has no `where`, because time does not visit one place.** It had one while it was a
# player's command; as a step of ending a turn it restores every count there is, and the pattern
# its `put` matches names no place at all. **Checked against the tests rather than assumed** - the
# four that refresh have one territory or one scout between them, so none of them could tell a
# per-place refresh from this one, and none ever needed to.

# **Ending a turn is the composite, and refresh is its whole body so far.** `spec/turn.md` has five
# steps - upkeep, then growth or starvation, then what expires, then what nature reclaims, and
# *time restores every count to the number that thing's kind declares* last. **Only the last is
# built**, and the other four are rules this order has room for rather than something this rule
# will grow clauses to do.
#
# **It takes no arguments**, so its parts supply constants and nothing is passed down from a parent
# to a child. That is the smallest thing that works; a part that needed its parent's argument would
# be a second kind of `{argument ...}`, and nothing needs one yet.

{rule id:4 name:refresh}

{input id:11 rule:refresh seq:1 name:what of:relation}
{input id:12 rule:refresh seq:2 name:trait of:trait}

{clause id:15 rule:refresh seq:1 role:put relation:unit name:clause-15}

{relation-of clause:clause-15 input:what}

{assigns id:1 clause:clause-15 input:trait value:1}

{rule id:5 name:end-turn}

# **Every part is one of `spec/turn.md`'s steps, in its order** - upkeep is paid, then a population
# grows on surplus food or starves for want of it, then what expires and what was not kept is lost,
# then what nature reclaims, and time restoring the counts last. **Only what nature reclaims is
# missing**, and `tree.txt` is where the order can be read.
#
# **`upkeep` and `perish` sat under a composite called `adjust-population` for a day**, and it was
# the wrong pair to group. Of its two parts one did not adjust the population - `upkeep` removes a
# citizen and adds a citizen - and the other only ever shrank it, so the name promised a symmetry
# that had no second half. Sean, 2026-09-20: *adjust-population is too vague, how is it adjusting
# population?*
#
# **And the grouping cut across the document it was meant to follow.** Eating is step one and
# starving is step two, *with* breeding - so `perish` belongs beside `breed` and not beside
# `upkeep`. **The grouping comes back as `grow-or-starve` when `breed` gives it a second part**;
# until then it would be a composite of one, which is two rows nothing distinguishes.
#
# **Hunger is restored like every other count**, by the same `refresh` the scouts and the
# extractors use. It is one rule over a trait, so a third pairing costs two argument rows and no
# vocabulary at all - which is what parameterising it bought.

{part id:4 of:end-turn is:upkeep seq:1 name:part-4}
{part id:6 of:end-turn is:perish seq:2 name:part-6}
{part id:7 of:end-turn is:breed seq:3 name:part-7}
{part id:3 of:end-turn is:discard-disorder seq:4 name:part-3}
{part id:1 of:end-turn is:refresh seq:5 name:part-1}
{part id:2 of:end-turn is:refresh seq:6 name:part-2}
{part id:5 of:end-turn is:refresh seq:7 name:part-5}
{part id:8 of:end-turn is:refresh seq:8 name:part-8}

{argument id:1 part:part-1 input:what value:unit}
{argument id:2 part:part-1 input:trait value:moving}
{argument id:3 part:part-2 input:what value:extractor}
{argument id:4 part:part-2 input:trait value:working}
{argument id:5 part:part-5 input:what value:citizen}
{argument id:6 part:part-5 input:trait value:hungry}
{argument id:7 part:part-8 input:what value:citizen}
{argument id:8 part:part-8 input:trait value:bearing}

# **`build-bin` says nothing about how many will fit**, the same way `build-extractor` says
# nothing about deposits. That a territory has room for only so many is a fact about the world,
# stated once in `schema.4x` and enforced wherever a world is checked - so a rule that cannot
# break it is better than a rule that remembers not to.

{rule id:6 name:build-bin}

{input id:13 rule:build-bin seq:1 name:where of:territory}
{input id:14 rule:build-bin seq:2 name:what of:resource}

