# **`discard-disorder` is `spec/turn.md`'s third step**: *what expires expires, and what was not
# kept in order is lost*. **It comes after the population is adjusted and before the refreshes**,
# which is the order that document states - upkeep, then growth, then this, then what nature
# reclaims, and time restoring the counts last.
#
# **It was called `discard-disorder`, which was that sentence**, and a rule's name being its
# own citation is worth something. **It cost more than it bought**: the turn's other steps are
# verbs - `upkeep`, `perish`, `refresh` - and this was the one clause, and by
# some way the longest thing a player reads in `tree.txt`. Sean, 2026-09-19, naming the concept it
# takes its name from instead: *everything over capacity is in disorder. There will be an end turn
# mechanic that discards everything in disorder.* **The sentence it was named for is right here**,
# one line up, which is where the citation lives now.
#
# **Its one clause has the role `keep`**, which bounds a quantity to the room there is for it. That
# is the fifth role and the first that reads a capacity: `require` and `remove` match, `add` makes,
# `put` assigns, and this one takes away what will not fit.

# **It takes no argument, and the sweep is what said so.** It had a `what`, and
# `{relation-of clause:clause-20 input:what}` to read it - but the clause already declares
# `relation:resource`, and `resource` is the only loose family, so the argument could only ever be
# what the clause already said. **Deleting the `relation-of` row broke nothing**, which is the
# sweep reporting decoration rather than a rule.
#
# **So the input, the `relation-of` and the argument are all gone**, and the rule says what it does.
# If a second family is ever loose, the argument comes back - and then it will be doing something.

{rule id:7 name:discard-disorder}

{clause id:20 rule:discard-disorder seq:1 role:keep relation:resource name:clause-20}

# **`upkeep` is `spec/turn.md`'s first step**: *upkeep is paid*. One citizen eats one food and is
# no longer hungry, and it repeats - so as many eat as there is food for, and nothing anywhere
# computes a minimum. Sean, 2026-09-19, on the loop he intends: *each citizen cosumes 1 food or
# perishes*.
#
# **It is scoped per territory**, which is what joins the citizen to the food. Without it the
# clause would name no place, match a citizen in every territory at once, and be refused for not
# having said which - `Refused::NotOneToTake`.
#
# **Both clauses name every trait they mean.** `hungry:1` is what makes one of the two citizen rows
# in a place the one meant, and it is why nothing here has to choose.

# **Its `add` reads the parent's `bearing` rather than stating one.** A citizen carries two traits
# now, and an `add` must name every column - so eating has to say what the fed citizen's `bearing`
# is. **Writing `1` would be right only because the turn restores it last**, and would quietly hand
# a free breeding to any citizen that ate with its bearing already spent.
#
# **So a `require` goes first, to be read from.** It is the shape `work` already uses to produce
# *the density here* rather than a number somebody typed, and it puts the coupling where it can
# fail loudly: two citizen rows differing in `bearing` make the reading ambiguous, and `NotOne`
# says so instead of the engine picking one.

{rule id:8 name:upkeep}

{input id:15 rule:upkeep seq:1 name:where of:territory}

{scope rule:upkeep input:where}
{repeats rule:upkeep}

{clause id:28 rule:upkeep seq:1 role:require relation:citizen name:clause-28}
{clause id:21 rule:upkeep seq:2 role:remove relation:citizen name:clause-21}
{clause id:22 rule:upkeep seq:3 role:remove relation:food name:clause-22}
{clause id:23 rule:upkeep seq:4 role:add relation:citizen name:clause-23}

{binding id:41 clause:clause-28 column:130 input:where}
{binding id:34 clause:clause-21 column:130 input:where}
{binding id:35 clause:clause-22 column:82 input:where}
{binding id:36 clause:clause-23 column:130 input:where}

{literal id:36 clause:clause-28 column:131 value:1}
{literal id:23 clause:clause-21 column:131 value:1}
{literal id:24 clause:clause-21 column:132 value:1}
{literal id:25 clause:clause-22 column:83 value:1}
{literal id:26 clause:clause-23 column:131 value:0}
{literal id:27 clause:clause-23 column:132 value:1}

{reading id:2 clause:clause-23 column:133 of:clause-28 takes:133}

# **`perish` is the consequence `hungry` does not carry.** A trait says what a thing may do; what
# happens to a thing that did not is a step of the turn, with a name a player can read.
#
# **It takes the row rather than one of them**, which is what a `remove` clause naming no quantity
# means: everyone still hungry after upkeep goes at once. **So its repetition fires once and then
# runs out**, and that is not what the repetition is for.
#
# **It repeats because *as many times as it can* is zero**, and a turn has to end in a world with
# no citizens in it. A bare `remove` refuses when nothing matches, which is right for a player's
# command and wrong for a step the turn fires whatever the world holds.
#
# **So every step of the turn is a no-op when there is nothing to do**, by one of three routes -
# `put` assigns nothing, `keep` finds nothing over, and a repetition fires zero times.
#
# **The quantity it used to name was decoration and the sweep said so.** With `repeats` the rule
# reaches the same world one at a time or all at once, so `{literal ... column:132 value:1}` could
# be deleted and nothing failed.

{rule id:9 name:perish}

{input id:16 rule:perish seq:1 name:where of:territory}

{scope rule:perish input:where}
{repeats rule:perish}

{clause id:24 rule:perish seq:1 role:remove relation:citizen name:clause-24}

{binding id:37 clause:clause-24 column:130 input:where}

{literal id:28 clause:clause-24 column:131 value:1}

# **`upkeep` before `perish` is where the order lives**, and it is the first time `part.seq` has
# carried an answer rather than a tidy arrangement. **Swap the two and everyone dies** - perish
# fired first finds every citizen hungry and the food goes untouched - which is what
# `the-hungry-perish-after-upkeep-and-not-before` asserts.
#
# **`breed` goes beside `perish` and is not built.** `backlog.md` carries it: a citizen that is not
# hungry and has a `bearing` makes another. It is the half of `spec/turn.md`'s second step that
# grows where `perish` shrinks, and keeping it out is what keeps this increment's tests about
# hunger.

# **`breed` is `spec/turn.md`'s second step, the half that grows**: *a population grows on surplus
# food or starves for want of it.* `perish` is the other half, and the two are neighbours in the
# turn because the document puts them in one step.
#
# **Excess is a position in the order and not a comparison.** Whatever food is standing when this
# runs is by construction the surplus - `upkeep` has already taken what the citizens owed - so
# nothing anywhere subtracts, and nothing asks whether there is more food than citizens.
#
# **The parent is consumed and returns as one of two.** That is what spends its `bearing` without a
# `put` beside a `remove`, and it is why no citizen breeds twice in a turn. Sean, 2026-09-19, on
# the loop: *each remaining (citizen, food) produces an additional citizen.*
#
# **So the increase is the smaller of the food to spare and the citizens that can bear**, and
# nothing computes a minimum: it repeats until one of the two clauses stops matching. Three
# citizens and ten food give six; three and five give five; three and two give two, because one
# starved and none bred.

{rule id:10 name:breed}

{input id:17 rule:breed seq:1 name:where of:territory}

{scope rule:breed input:where}
{repeats rule:breed}

{clause id:25 rule:breed seq:1 role:remove relation:citizen name:clause-25}
{clause id:26 rule:breed seq:2 role:remove relation:food name:clause-26}
{clause id:27 rule:breed seq:3 role:add relation:citizen name:clause-27}

{binding id:38 clause:clause-25 column:130 input:where}
{binding id:39 clause:clause-26 column:82 input:where}
{binding id:40 clause:clause-27 column:130 input:where}

